{"kind":"report","text":"changes-requested. Independent review of the supported-host posture at exact head 8b427acf24d6f87da89d469709ab6496a017793c, which fast-forwards from main afa911ea, reviewed in a fresh detached worktree restored and verified clean after every experiment.\n\nGATES, all at the exact head and uncached: gofmt clean; go build ./... ok; go vet ./... ok; go test -count=1 ./... ok with no cached packages and internal/docset ok; go test -race -count=1 ./... ok with no data races; ui npm ci then npm test 60/60; make ui-check rebuilds internal/service/uidist byte-identically to the committed embed. internal/kernel, internal/gitstore, internal/app, internal/workroom and internal/custody trees are byte-identical to afa911ea, proved by tree hash rather than by assertion.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layers are 3, Nexus and live runtime (docs/reference/architecture.md:137), and 7, CLI, MCP, skills, connectors and UI (:524), plus the internal/app row (:590). The layer 1 and 2 kernel contract is unchanged and the page states that twice (:173, :590). The internal/custody row (:584) is corrected from the false \"Manages local keys and migrations above the kernel\" to an interpreter description; that is right, because internal/custody declares no key, signing or migration identifier and is byte-unchanged here, so the old row was always wrong. Contracts preserved.\n\nSECURITY. Ruled acceptable in design. The acknowledgement is checked at cmd/gs/main.go:1667, after validateLoopbackListen at :1664 and before app.Open at :1671, so no repository is opened and no address published without it. Every mutating route reaches side effects only through decode, which calls guardMutation first (internal/service/server.go:758-760), and TrustedHostHandler (:705) wraps the mux ahead of routing (cmd/gs/main.go:1721); all sixteen POST handlers decode first and no GET route mutates, handleRebuild being a progress read. No Access-Control-* header is ever emitted. Credential entropy is crypto/rand, 32 bytes and so 256 bits; nexus.go imports crypto/rand only and math/rand appears nowhere in the tree. The credential is only ever a map key: presenceEntry carries no credential field, snapshotLocked keys by handle, Change.ID is the handle, and InboxFrame.Recipients are fingerprints. It left the URL when departure moved to POST /v0/presence/depart, which is what closes the route-template telemetry and referrer sinks. On timing, there is no subtle.ConstantTimeCompare and none is needed: the secret is never compared by string equality against a stored secret, lookup is a Go map index, and the only string comparisons are on non-secret actor and fingerprint values. Every refusal is a fixed string. The browser keeps the value in a ref and React state and never in localStorage, sessionStorage, cookies, indexedDB or the history API; only \"workroom.actor\", a name, is persisted. TrustedHostHandler exempts GET and HEAD while GET /v0/status serves the whole projection, but a rebound page can only issue that request and not read it, since no CORS header is sent and no GET route mutates, so I rule that acceptable at this head.\n\nMUTATIONS. Eighteen guards mutated one at a time. Caught: acknowledgement bypass; literal non-loopback --listen; cross-actor renewal binding; a credential echoed into a refusal; the trusted-host guard bypassed three ways, loopbackRequestHost forced true, TrustedHostHandler made a no-op, and both against the whole service suite; the Origin scheme and shape check; the JSON content-type gate; revocation not deleting the lease; the credential reused as its own public handle; credential entropy cut from 32 to 4 bytes; and the credential injected into the whoami tool result. Three survived, and two of them are the changes I am requesting.\n\nCHANGES REQUESTED\n\n1. The multi-resolution listener rule has no test that bites. Rewriting the resolution loop in cmd/gs/main.go from \"refuse if any resolved address is non-loopback\" to \"accept if any resolved address is loopback\" leaves the entire cmd/gs package green. TestValidateLoopbackListen covers literals and \"localhost\", whose addresses are all loopback, so the mixed case the conditions name is never exercised. This guards the highest-consequence invariant in the change: the acknowledged posture is that everything inside the boundary can act as every actor key, so a regression that binds a routable interface hands the whole workroom to the network and nothing goes red. Add a resolver seam, a package-level var lookupIP = net.LookupIP overridden in the test, and a case where one name resolves to both 127.0.0.1 and a non-loopback address and is refused.\n\n2. The browser has no re-mint path and wedges permanently. In ui/src/lib/session.ts the renew callback clears credentialRef only when the effect re-runs; its catch sets live false and leaves the dead value in place, and json() in ui/src/lib/api.ts throws on the 400 a dead credential earns. After a resident restart, a revocation, or any lapse past the 30 second TTL, the tab re-sends the same dead credential every 15 seconds for ever and never recovers without a manual reload. At afa911ea the browser minted its own identifier and the same heartbeat re-announced it, so this is a regression against preserved behaviour rather than a pre-existing gap, and the MCP adapter already handles exactly this case in announceUpdate, which clears the lease on \"credential is not valid\" and re-announces. No page documents a reload as the workaround. Clear credentialRef and the exposed id on that refusal so the next tick re-mints, and cover it in ui/test.\n\n3. Smaller, please fold in with the above. Non-renewed expiry is only proved through the explicit Expire API: TestCredentialsAreResidentScopedAndExpire calls Expire before renewing, so deleting h.expire from RenewSessionIdentity still passes. Low consequence, since eighteen other entry points sweep, but the conditions name it; advance a clock into the renew path instead. And docs/reference/gs/serve.md:180 lists only \"--listen must name a loopback address\" and omits the second refusal this head introduces, \"--listen must resolve only to loopback addresses\", which is what a name that fails to resolve or resolves off-loopback now returns; the same section should note that --listen localhost:7777 now goes through net.LookupIP instead of the removed EqualFold short-circuit and can therefore newly fail when resolution fails.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. validCredential is dead weight for security: both call sites run it after the map lookup, so it saves nothing, and forcing it to true leaves the nexus suite green, because a value that is not a minted credential can never be a key in the presence map. If it stays as a shape pre-filter, move it before the lookup so it does the job its comment claims; otherwise drop it and the coupling to the two constants. Session.id in the UI is now a 256-bit secret behind a name that invites rendering; it currently only ever reaches request bodies and never the DOM, but the next component that renders a session id leaks it, so rename it credential or keep only the ref and expose live. The guard for that in ui/test/interaction.test.mjs matches the literal word \"credential\" on a localStorage.setItem line and would not catch storing the value under another name. Hub.Expire is exported with no caller outside the package's own tests.\n\nI explicitly reject adding any partial authentication here. A credential scoped to a loopback port cannot protect against a same-account peer process, which can read the operator's key material directly, and a framework that looked like authentication would earn trust the boundary cannot repay. What this change actually buys is protection against the web attacker, a hostile page in the operator's browser, and the acknowledgement flag, the loopback rule, the Host, Origin and Sec-Fetch-Site guards and the absence of CORS are exactly the right shape for that threat with the residual honestly disclosed in the flag text, the startup banner, status.trust_boundary, the join gate and the docs. Do not grow it into a login.\n\nRe-review is only the delta: fix 1 and 2, ideally 3, publish a fresh artifact at the new head, and I will re-run the mutation seams rather than repeat the whole review.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:b47444c45caefc9084315b7ed4cdff2c405b17b1","head":"8b427acf24d6f87da89d469709ab6496a017793c","stale":"true","staleness":"artifact, promise, request stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0c15ae61a553e3f65a036087b157fb47010f3793, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:22e522fe58eef152ef3ad253c86a11a51db03532, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:23c336b4c36d39576422c1f2ec6171cb1810679f, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:29439846217f934baf0582e15ab6814bf6ca37c8 and 20 more","verdict":"changes-requested"}}